from author_common import *
T=[
(229,'1971-09-06','A. A. Kirillov','P000360-P000364','Prequantization and polarizations','talk',r'The actual heading on P000360 begins a five-page exposition of Kostant’s geometric quantization: symplectic and Hamiltonian vector fields, integral curvature, line bundles with connection, the prequantum representation, and polarized sections. The notebook’s factors of two and signs do not fit one convention throughout. The supplied catalogue span P000358--P000360 includes the end of the preceding year’s Perelomov discussion and omits most of this talk; those boundary pages are not new occurrences of record 229.'),
(230,'','1971–72','P000365','Academic-year heading','heading',r'This is the catalogue’s academic-year heading, retained as a record in its own right. The supplied range P000358--P000366 overlaps substantive records; no independent mathematical assertion is attached to the heading. P000365 is an ordering anchor before the 13 September talks, not a claim that its scan contains a separate handwritten year heading.'),
(231,'1971-09-13','I. M. Gelfand','P000365-P000366','Springer resolution, flags, and Hall polynomials','talk',r'Gelfand describes Springer’s resolution of the unipotent variety and the geometry of invariant complete flags. The finite-field flag counts connect Hall polynomials with the even cohomology of flag varieties; a centralizer formula bounds the dimensions of Springer fibres. The literal claim about arbitrary reductive groups over every field needs geometric and characteristic qualifications.'),
(232,'1971-09-13','B. I. Rosenfeld','P000366','Derivations of differential forms and an unidentified note by Rego','talk',r'The lower part of P000366 reports a note attributed to Rego in volume 272 of the Comptes rendus and recalls the Frölicher--Nijenhuis description of graded derivations. The named note and its proposed cohomology theory were not identified securely. The displayed calculus can nevertheless be reconstructed without inventing the missing result.'),
(233,'1971-09-13','Vlastimil Pták','P000367','Critical exponents of finite-dimensional normed spaces','talk',r'The talk asks how many consecutive norm-preserving powers of a contraction force spectral radius one. It records exact Hilbert and polyhedral examples, a two-dimensional counterexample, questions about \(\ell_p^n\), the minimum over all norms, and a constrained spectral-radius extremum. A 1971 paper already answers the rational-\(p\) finiteness question more generally than the notebook acknowledges; the exact later fate of the other stated questions was not established.'),
(235,'1971-09-13','I. M. Gelfand','P000368','The affine Radon transform over a finite field','talk',r'Gelfand derives inversion by counting affine hyperplanes through one or two points and identifies a parallel-class range condition. A final remark connects finite-field subspace counts to Gaussian binomial coefficients and their \(q\to1\) limit. This heading precedes Kirillov’s P000369 heading despite the opposite order of catalogue record numbers.'),
(234,'1971-09-13','A. A. Kirillov','P000369','Wavefront sets and oscillatory integrals','talk',r'The one-page talk introduces the wavefront set, conormal Lagrangians, pseudodifferential kernels, and oscillatory representations. Hörmander’s 1971 paper provides a close contemporary source. The notebook omits localization in its wavefront criterion and overstates the converse from wavefront containment to membership in a Lagrangian distribution class.'),
(236,'1971-09-20','I. N. Bernstein','P000370','The Godbillon–Vey class','talk',r'Bernstein explains the three-form attached to a cooriented codimension-one foliation, a homogeneous nonzero example, and the relation to formal vector-field cohomology. The claim of stability under deformation requires correction: arbitrary smooth families can change the Godbillon--Vey class. Atiyah’s talk starts later on the same page.'),
(237,'1971-09-20','Michael Atiyah','P000370-P000373','Spectral flow and an index on a mapping torus','talk',r'Atiyah describes the integer obtained by following eigenvalues of a self-adjoint elliptic family and identifies it with the index of a suspended operator. A circle example, an eigenvalue-orbit argument, and remarks about diffeomorphisms and higher signatures follow. The main idea subsequently appeared in the third Atiyah--Patodi--Singer paper; the notebook’s derivative estimate and unitary conjugation sign need repair.'),
(238,'1971-09-20','Pierre Deligne; translated by Yu. I. Manin','P000373','Motives: a two-line notice','talk',r'The entry says only that Deligne, with Manin translating, spoke about motives and the various cohomology theories of algebraic varieties. It records no theorem, construction, or conjecture sufficiently specific for a publication-level identification.'),
(239,'1971-10-25','S. I. Gelfand','P000373-P000375','Schubert cells and invariant characteristic forms','talk',r'The talk compares the Schubert decomposition of a complex Grassmannian with invariant differential forms, curvature polynomials, and Schur functions. A separate intervention by I. M. Gelfand asks broadly about a defining-characteristic group algebra for \(SL_2(\mathbb F_q)\); the intended classification target is not specified. Vinogradov’s talk starts on the lower part of P000375.'),
(240,'1971-10-25','A. M. Vinogradov','P000375-P000377','Differential operators as functors over a ring','talk',r'Vinogradov defines order by iterated commutators with multiplication and introduces the left and right module structures underlying jets, cojets, and higher derivations. His 1972 announcement gives the coherent version over a commutative unital ring. The notebook’s apparent permission to take an arbitrary noncommutative ring cannot be carried through unchanged.'),
(241,'1971-11-01','A. M. Vinogradov','P000377-P000383','Jets, higher derivations, and algebraic differential complexes','talk',r'The second lecture develops universal jet modules, the recursive derivation functors, symbols, and de Rham and Spencer constructions. The characteristic-two discussion must distinguish skew symmetry from alternation. A literal example using only the prime field \(\mathbb F_2\) cannot support nonzero derivations; nearby dual-number and group-algebra examples are mathematically different.'),
(242,'1971-11-08','A. M. Vinogradov','P000384-P000386','Six lemmas on differential operators and cojets','talk',r'The third lecture proves the elementary identities behind the differential-operator functors: pre- and post-composition, the first-order product identity, addition of orders under composition, and the universal evaluation/currying adjunction. These are the technical foundations of the 1972 announcement, with a commutative base ring understood.'),
(243,'1971-11-15','I. M. Gelfand','P000387-P000394','Formal coordinate geometry and characteristic classes of foliations','talk',r'Gelfand develops the infinite frame bundle, its formal vector-field coframing, relative Lie algebra cohomology, and characteristic classes; Bernstein intervenes on inverse-limit manifolds. The final application is Bott’s obstruction to integrability, already discussed in the preceding year. The printed truncation threshold and the homogeneous degrees of Chern generators need correction. Arnold’s new heading begins later on P000394 and is excluded from this record.')]
talks([dict(zip(['record','date','speaker','pages','title','kind','summary'],x)) for x in T])
K=['kostant1970quantization']
items(229,[
I('P000360','definition','Symplectic form, Darboux coordinates, and Hamiltonian fields',r'The notes take a closed nondegenerate two-form \(\omega\) on a \(2n\)-manifold, write \(\omega=\sum dp_j\wedge dq_j\), and identify vector fields and one-forms. The coordinate identification and the names of locally Hamiltonian and Hamiltonian fields are not consistent with the displayed contraction.',r'On a smooth symplectic manifold the map \(X\mapsto\iota_X\omega\) is an isomorphism. A vector field preserves \(\omega\) exactly when \(d\iota_X\omega=0\); it is Hamiltonian, in a stated sign convention, when that one-form is globally exact. Darboux coordinates exist locally, and \(\omega^n/n!\) is a nonvanishing volume form.',r'These are established symplectic preliminaries in \citet[geometric preliminaries]{kostant1970quantization}, published in 1970; the seminar is expository here.',r'the distinction between local closedness and global exactness remains essential. The obstruction to a symplectic field being Hamiltonian is its class in \(H^1_{\mathrm{dR}}(M)\).',reconstruction=r'Use the usual unnormalised exterior product and state a contraction sign explicitly; this changes the notebook’s coordinate signs and absorbs its factor of two.',refs=K),
I('P000361','construction','Poisson bracket and the normalization problem',r'The notebook sets \(dF=2\alpha_{\xi_F}\), defines \(\{F,G\}=\xi_FG\), and also identifies this with \(2\omega(\xi_F,\xi_G)\). It explicitly allows a wedge convention with \((dp\wedge dq)(\partial_p,\partial_q)=1/2\).',r'Fix \(\iota_{X_f}\omega=-df\) and \(\{f,g\}=X_fg\). Then \(\{f,g\}=\omega(X_f,X_g)\), the bracket is a Lie bracket and a derivation in each argument, and \([X_f,X_g]=X_{\{f,g\}}\). These identities must all use the same normalization.',r'The Poisson Lie algebra is part of the prequantization framework already developed in \citet{kostant1970quantization}. The discrepancy is internal to the recorded formulas, not a later mathematical obstruction.',r'the construction is standard; the notebook’s simultaneous sign and normalization choices cannot be adopted literally without alteration.',literal_correctness='false as written',reconstruction=r'The displayed convention is an editorial normalization, not a claim about Kirillov’s exact intended sign.',refs=K),
I('P000361-P000362','theorem','Integral symplectic forms and prequantum line bundles',r'Local primitives \(\alpha_j\), differences \(df_{jk}\), integral triple overlaps, and transition functions \(e^{2\pi i f_{jk}}\) construct a line bundle.',r'A closed real two-form \(\omega\) is the curvature class of a Hermitian line bundle with unitary connection of curvature \(-2\pi i\omega\) if and only if \([\omega]\) lies in the image of \(H^2(M;\mathbb Z)\to H^2_{\mathrm{dR}}(M;\mathbb R)\). Local potentials and a good cover give the Čech construction.',r'This is the prequantization integrality criterion of \citet{kostant1970quantization}, published before the talk. The notes’ line-bundle construction is an exact conceptual match.',r'the theorem is established. Torsion in integral cohomology is invisible to the de Rham class and contributes to the choice of prequantization.',publication_match='exact',refs=K),
I('P000362','claim','Ambiguity of prequantization and flat twists',r'The ambiguity in the line-bundle construction is described using \(H^1(X,\mathbb C^*)\).',r'Isomorphism classes of line bundles with connection of a fixed curvature, when nonempty, form a torsor for flat line bundles with connection. In the unitary setting this group is \(H^1(M;\mathbb R/\mathbb Z)\), equivalently homomorphisms \(\pi_1(M)\to U(1)\); for complex flat connections use the locally constant sheaf \(\mathbb C^*\).',r'The ambiguity is part of the classification in \citet{kostant1970quantization}. The sheaf and connection data are necessary to interpret the abbreviated notation.',r'one must not confuse this torsor with the classification of bare smooth complex line bundles by \(H^2(M;\mathbb Z)\).',reconstruction=r'The constant-sheaf interpretation and the distinction between unitary and general complex flat bundles are supplied explicitly.',refs=K),
I('P000362-P000363','construction','The prequantum representation by first-order operators',r'Compatible scalar first-order operators are expressed through a connection and multiplication. The notes give \(D_\varphi=\nabla_{\xi_\varphi}+\pi i\varphi\) and a Poisson commutator formula.',r'With \(\iota_{X_f}\omega=-df\) and \(F_\nabla=-2\pi i\omega\), the operators \(D_f=\nabla_{X_f}+2\pi i f\) satisfy \([D_f,D_g]=D_{\{f,g\}}\). They are first-order operators on smooth sections of the prequantum line bundle; suitable domain and completeness assumptions are needed to exponentiate to group representations.',r'\citet{kostant1970quantization} develops this representation before the seminar. The notebook explicitly cites the 1970 lecture volume; it is not an unpublished new quantization theorem.',r'the Lie-algebra representation is standard, while its analytic integration and the choice of a smaller quantum space are separate questions.',reconstruction=r'The coefficients in the modern formula follow the normalization fixed in item 1971-229.2. No literal equality with the notebook’s \(\pi i\) convention is asserted.',refs=K),
I('P000363','definition','Real, complex, and mixed polarizations',r'A polarization is an involutive complex Lagrangian distribution \(F\). The cases \(F=\bar F\) and \(F\cap\bar F=0\) are called real and Kähler; mixed polarizations require a constant-rank condition. A sphere is said not to admit a global polarization.',r'A regular polarization is a complex rank-\(n\) involutive Lagrangian subbundle of \(T_\mathbb C M\). A Kähler polarization additionally has the appropriate definiteness of \(i\omega(v,\bar v)\). On \(S^2\), no nonsingular real line-field polarization exists, but the standard complex structure gives a Kähler polarization.',r'Polarizations and their use in quantization are developed in \citet{kostant1970quantization}. The sphere remark is correct only for nonsingular real polarizations, not for all complex polarizations.',r'positivity, regularity, and the possible singularities of a real polarization must be specified. Transversality to the conjugate distribution alone gives a compatible complex structure, not positivity.',reconstruction=r'The restriction of the sphere claim to the real case is inferred from its local context and is flagged rather than silently supplied.',refs=K),
I('P000364','theorem','Polarized sections and the Poisson normalizer',r'The quantum space is written \(\Gamma(L,F)=\{s:\nabla_Xs=0\text{ for }X\in F\}\). Leafwise constant functions form a locally maximal Poisson-commutative algebra \(A(F)\); its normalizer acts by first-order operators.',r'For a regular real polarization, locally in a foliated Darboux chart, \(A(F)\) is maximal Poisson-commutative. If \(X_f\) preserves \(F\), the prequantum operator \(D_f\) preserves polarized sections. This preservation condition is locally equivalent to \(\{f,A(F)\}\subset A(F)\). Global maximality requires enough global first integrals and is not automatic.',r'This is the standard polarization-preserving observable construction of \citet{kostant1970quantization}. The notebook itself qualifies maximal commutativity by ``locally.''',r'the geometric statement is established. Global polarized sections may be too small or vanish, and half-form and Hilbert-space choices are additional structures, not supplied by this page.',refs=K),
I('P000364','example','Vertical polarization on a cotangent bundle',r'For the distribution \(dq=0\), functions of \(q\) act by multiplication and functions linear in \(p\) give first-order operators.',r'On \(T^*Q\) with its vertical real polarization, local polarized sections depend only on \(q\). The functions preserving this polarization are locally \(f(q,p)=a^j(q)p_j+b(q)\); their prequantum operators descend to first-order differential operators on \(Q\), with constants and signs fixed by the chosen connection.',r'This Schrödinger-model example is part of the earlier construction in \citet{kostant1970quantization}. The affine, rather than strictly homogeneous linear, dependence on \(p\) includes multiplication operators.',r'the local assertion is standard; a global measure or half-density convention determines the additional divergence term in a symmetric operator realization.',kind='construction' if False else 'example',refs=K)
])
